import Foundation

// MARK: - Starting Over Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let startingOverContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 43:18-19",
            scriptureText: "Forget the former things; do not dwell on the past. See, I am doing a new thing! Now it springs up; do you not perceive it? I am making a way in the wilderness and streams in the wasteland.",
            devotionalTitle: "The God of New Things",
            devotionalText: "God doesn't just clean up old messes — He creates entirely new things. He makes roads where there are none and rivers in deserts. If your past feels like a wasteland, look closer. God is already doing something new. The question isn't whether He can — it's whether you can perceive it. Stop staring at what was and start looking for what's springing up.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I choose to stop dwelling on my past. I look forward to the new thing You are doing. Open my eyes to see it — the road in the wilderness, the stream in the wasteland. I trust that my past does not define my future because You are the God of new beginnings. Spring up something fresh in my life today. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What new thing might God be doing in your life that you haven't noticed yet?",
            actionSteps: ["Write down one thing from your past you need to stop dwelling on and symbolically release it", "Look for one sign of God doing something new in your life and thank Him for it"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Lamentations 3:22-24",
            scriptureText: "Because of the Lord's great love we are not consumed, because his compassions never fail. They are new every morning; great is your faithfulness. I say to myself, 'The Lord is my portion; therefore I will wait for him.'",
            devotionalTitle: "New Every Morning",
            devotionalText: "Every sunrise is God's way of saying 'let's try again.' His mercies don't carry over from yesterday — they're freshly minted each morning. If yesterday was a disaster, today is a clean page. God's faithfulness isn't based on your performance; it's based on His character. You woke up today, which means fresh mercy is already waiting.",
            prayerText: "Lord, thank You for new mercies this morning. Yesterday is gone, and today is fresh. I am not consumed because of Your great love. Your compassion never fails — not yesterday, not today, not tomorrow. You are my portion, and that is enough. Help me start again with confidence in Your faithfulness. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What do you need fresh mercy for this morning?",
            actionSteps: ["Start tomorrow by thanking God for new mercies before you think about yesterday's failures", "Write 'New mercies today' on something you'll see first thing in the morning"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ezekiel 37:1-6",
            scriptureText: "The hand of the Lord was on me, and he brought me out by the Spirit of the Lord and set me in the middle of a valley of dry bones... 'I will make breath enter you, and you will come to life.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Life From Dry Bones",
            devotionalText: "God took Ezekiel to a valley of dead, dry bones — the most hopeless scene imaginable — and asked, 'Can these bones live?' The answer wasn't about the bones; it was about God. He breathes life into dead things. Your failures, addictions, broken relationships, shattered dreams — God looks at the driest parts of your life and says 'I will make these live again.'",
            prayerText: "Lord, I stand in a valley of dry bones. Parts of my life feel dead and beyond repair. But I believe You can breathe life into anything. Speak to these dry bones. Make them live again. I trust that nothing is too far gone for You. Breathe Your Spirit into the deadest parts of my life. In Jesus' name, amen.",
